Optimal Solution - In-place matrix zeroing using the first row and column as markers for constant space optimization.
                   Reuse the first row and column as marker arrays, and handle them carefully using separate flags to avoid overwriting original information.

class Solution {
    public void setZeroes(int[][] matrix) {
        // Flags to track whether first row or first column should be zeroed
        boolean firstRow = false, firstCol = false;
        // Step 1: Use first row and first column as markers
        for (int i = 0; i < matrix.length; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < matrix[0].length; j++) {
                // If a zero is found
                if (matrix[i][j] == 0) {
                    // Mark if the zero is in first row
                    if (i == 0)
                        firstRow = true;
                    // Mark if the zero is in first column
                    if (j == 0)
                        firstCol = true;
                    // Mark corresponding row and column in first row/column
                    matrix[0][j] = 0;
                    matrix[i][0] = 0;
                }
            }
        }
        // Step 2: Set cells to zero based on markers (excluding first row & column)
        for (int i = 1; i < matrix.length; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j < matrix[0].length; j++) {
                // If either row or column is marked
                if (matrix[i][0] == 0 || matrix[0][j] == 0) {
                    matrix[i][j] = 0;
                }
            }
        }
        // Step 3: Zero out the first row if needed
        if (firstRow) {
            for (int j = 0; j < matrix[0].length; j++) {
                matrix[0][j] = 0;
            }
        }
        // Step 4: Zero out the first column if needed
        if (firstCol) {
            for (int i = 0; i < matrix.length; i++) {
                matrix[i][0] = 0;
            }
        }
    }
}

Time Complexity :- O(m * n).
Space Complexity :- O(1).
